      "problem": "$Γ(\\frac{1}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=$",
      "worked_solution": "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nΓ(\\frac{1}{2})+Γ(\\frac{3}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$",
